{"purpose":"Capture the release-coordination intent for the EKA ecosystem: every repository gets a release pipeline (eka-core currently has none), releases follow a documented order (standard → core → cli → mcp), and version-sensitive knowledge (std:eka-standard-1.0.0, CLI standardVersion, embedded EKA file) is bumped and kept in sync when the standard evolves — so dependent units (eka-mcp-production, team-collaboration) are not blocked by missing release mechanics.","content":"## 1. Background\n\nThe EKA ecosystem ships four repositories: eka-standard (specification), eka-core (library), eka-cli (binary), eka-mcp (binary). Release pipelines: eka-standard, eka-cli, and eka-mcp already have tag-driven release workflows (eka-mcp's release.yml ships binaries + SHA256SUMS.txt + GitHub release, delivered by sto:mcp-release-ci, done); **eka-core has only ci.yml — no tag-driven release workflow, no release notes automation** — the single missing pipeline. Two approved units depend on new eka-core releases: eka-mcp-production (sto:mcp-contract-relocation moves the plugin contract into eka-core/plugin) and team-collaboration (conformance changes: mbr- token, assigned-to vocabulary, R13 sub-check — already scoped in sto:assigned-work-core). Independently, the standard is about to evolve: team-collaboration adds the 28th token (mbr-), which bumps EKA Standard to 1.1 — touching the eka-standard repository (EKA file, tokens/overview.md 27→28, new tokens/mbr.md), the std CKO (std:eka-standard-1.0.0 declares 27 tokens), the CLI standardVersion (currently derived from eka-core's exchange.SpecificationVersion constant — NOT from an embedded file), and eka-core's conformance enforcement (27-token tables in state.go/domain.go/cko.go plus len==27 tests). Nothing currently owns: (a) the eka-core release pipeline, (b) the release order, (c) the standard-version bump coordination across all four layers. Audit 2026-08-15 (session eka-mcp-production).\n\n## 2. Users and needs\n\n- As an operator, I want eka-core to have a tag-driven release pipeline (mirroring the eka-cli/eka-mcp pattern, adapted for a library), so a new release is produced consistently without manual steps.\n- As an operator, I want a documented release order (standard → core → cli → mcp), so dependent consumers always build against a released dependency, never source or pins.\n- As an operator, I want the standard version to be a single coordinated bump (standard repo, std CKO, eka-core SpecificationVersion, CLI standardVersion), so the ecosystem reports one standard after the coordinated change set lands.\n- As an implementer of eka-mcp-production, I want the eka-core release with the plugin package to exist before eka-mcp drops its eka-cli dependency, so the A2 relocation resolves the release-cadence lock for good.\n\n## 3. Locked decisions (planning discussion 2026-08-15, evidence cmt:release-coordination-implementation)\n\n1. **eka-core release pipeline** — add a release workflow to eka-core (tag-driven: vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH), producing a GitHub release with notes. Library-specific: no binaries/checksums needed (Go module consumers resolve from the tag; integrity via go.sum + sumdb). Guardrails (eka-core has no anvil.yaml, so the eka-cli/eka-mcp guardrail pattern is adapted): semver regex on the tag; duplicate-release check (gh release view fails fast); quality gate (gofmt/vet/test) inside the workflow before release creation; release notes via --generate-notes.\n2. **Go module rules (pinned)** — (a) never delete or re-create a pushed tag (proxy.golang.org caches immutably; a broken tag is broken forever for GOPROXY consumers) — trial releases use -rc suffixes; (b) no major bump (v2.0.0) without the /v2 module-path change; (c) every consumer release step MUST include an explicit go.mod bump (go get) — releasing a consumer does not automatically pick up a new core version (MVS); (d) the release workflow runs its own quality gate (ci.yml triggers only on branch/PR, not tags).\n3. **Release order** — one-way: eka-standard → eka-core → eka-cli → eka-mcp, consistent with req:prd dependency direction. A consumer release only happens after its dependency's release is published. The order is documented here and enforced by the plan's work items (each release step is a separate work item whose precondition is the upstream release; consumer release steps validate the upstream release exists — e.g. gh release view or the imported constant/asset).\n4. **Standard 1.1 bump coordination** — one coordinated change set, not four independent edits: (a) eka-standard repository: EKA file (Version 1.1, 28 tokens), tokens/overview.md (27→28 + mbr- row), new tokens/mbr.md, CHANGELOG — released as standard 1.1 via its existing release gate; (b) a NEW std CKO artifact (id eka-standard-1.1.0) with an `amends` relationship to std:eka-standard-1.0.0 (std is not a versioned type; a new artifact with amends is the correct EKA mechanism, P7 forward-only); (c) eka-core: exchange.SpecificationVersion bumped to \"1.1\" and the conformance 27-token enforcement updated (state.go/domain.go token tables, cko.go error text, len==27 tests, export_test.go) — this is the primary enforcement layer and is also scoped in team-collaboration's sto:assigned-work-core (the two must land in the same eka-core release); (d) eka-cli: standardVersion follows eka-core's constant automatically (exchange.SpecificationVersion) — no separate CLI source change; if sto:init-standard-declaration's embed of the EKA file lands first (cli-experience container), the embedded file must be refreshed in the same change set; (e) the version-consistency test is extended to cover the std CKO and the standard repo's EKA file. Transitional divergence during the release window (standard 1.1 released while CLI still reports 1.0) is expected and documented; \"in sync\" is defined as the state after the coordinated change set lands.\n5. **requiresEka is NOT a standard axis** — it is the minimum CLI version the pack is written against (ADR-023 D5); it is refreshed as part of CLI release steps (pack written against the new CLI), not part of the standard bump. It stays out of this bump set.\n6. **Roadmap position** — this unit executes in parallel readiness with the units it unblocks: the eka-core pipeline work item can start immediately; the actual eka-core release with the plugin package is gated on sto:mcp-contract-relocation (eka-mcp-production container); the standard 1.1 bump is gated on team-collaboration's standard changes. The release-coordination container is planned; activation order is the executor's decision, honoring these dependencies.\n\n## 4. Scope IN\n\n1. eka-core release pipeline: release.yml (tag-driven, GitHub release + notes, semver check, duplicate-release check, quality gate inside the workflow), release-process documentation in eka-core.\n2. Release order documentation: the one-way order (standard → core → cli → mcp) recorded here and referenced from each release work item, with upstream-release-exists validation as a step in consumer releases.\n3. Standard 1.1 bump execution (gated on team-collaboration standard changes): eka-standard repo artifacts (EKA file, tokens/overview.md, tokens/mbr.md, CHANGELOG) + new std CKO eka-standard-1.1.0 (amends eka-standard-1.0.0) + eka-core SpecificationVersion \"1.1\" and 27→28 conformance enforcement (coordinated with sto:assigned-work-core, same eka-core release) + version-consistency test extension.\n4. eka-core release run (gated on sto:mcp-contract-relocation): cut the first release containing the eka-core/plugin package; verify consumers (eka-cli, eka-mcp) build against the released version via explicit go.mod bumps (Go module rule 2c).\n\n## 5. Non-goals (locked)\n\nOut of scope: binary signing/checksum for eka-core (library — not applicable; integrity via go.sum + sumdb); changelog automation tooling beyond GitHub release notes; a monorepo/release-orchestration service; automating cross-repo release chaining (each repo releases independently; the order is a policy with manual validation steps, not a pipeline); requiresEka refresh as part of the standard bump (CLI-axis concern, see §3.5); FEEDBACK_ISSUE_TOKEN hardening (deferred); changes to the existing eka-standard/eka-cli/eka-mcp release workflows themselves (they are correct); anything that preempts plugin-install-trust (active) or cli-experience (planned) containers.\n\n## 6. Acceptance boundaries\n\n- eka-core has a release workflow triggered by a version tag; a test tag (rc suffix) produces a GitHub release with notes; duplicate tag/version refuses; quality gate runs inside the workflow; pushed tags are never deleted/recreated.\n- The release order (standard → core → cli → mcp) is documented here; each consumer release work item includes an upstream-release-exists validation step and an explicit go.mod bump.\n- After team-collaboration's standard changes land: eka-standard released 1.1 (EKA file, overview 28 tokens, mbr.md); std CKO eka-standard-1.1.0 exists with amends to eka-standard-1.0.0; eka-core SpecificationVersion = \"1.1\" and conformance accepts mbr- (tables + tests updated, 28 tokens); eka-cli standardVersion reports 1.1 (via the core constant, or the embedded file if sto:init-standard-declaration landed first — refreshed in the same set); version-consistency test passes over std CKO + standard repo EKA file.\n- After sto:mcp-contract-relocation lands: eka-core (with eka-core/plugin) is released; eka-cli and eka-mcp build against the released version via explicit go.mod bumps, not source/pins.\n- No container is activated prematurely; activation order respects the gating in §3.6; requiresEka stays on the CLI axis (not bumped with the standard).\n\n## 7. Evidence\n\n- cmt:release-coordination-implementation — planning discussion 2026-08-15.\n- Audit 2026-08-15 (session eka-mcp-production): eka-standard/eka-cli/eka-mcp have release workflows (eka-mcp's delivered by sto:mcp-release-ci, done); eka-core has only ci.yml.\n- Verified mechanics: eka-cli cmd/version.go derives standardVersion from eka-core's exchange.SpecificationVersion (no embedded file in the implementation today); eka-core conformance hardcodes 27 tokens (state.go, domain.go, cko.go, len==27 tests).\n- Dependencies: req:eka-mcp-production (A2 contract relocation → eka-core release), req:team-collaboration (mbr- token → standard 1.1; sto:assigned-work-core carries the eka-core conformance change), scp:cli-experience (standardVersion contract)."}